"Bayer ditches 1 AAV gene therapy from Pompe disease pair (https://www.fiercebiotech.com/biotech/bayer-ditches-one-aav-gene-therapy-pompe-disease-focus-rival-option)\nciteturn686view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.fiercebiotech.com/biotech/bayer-ditches-one-aav-gene-therapy-pompe-disease-focus-rival-option\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 40\nL0: 【0†Subscribe】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Image: Hamburger Menu】 【2†Image: Hamburger Menu】\nL3: \nL4: [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: 【3†Biotech】\nL7: # Bayer tosses 1 Pompe gene therapy from $2B AskBio buyout to focus on alternative option\nL8: \nL9: By 【4†James Waldron】 Mar 4, 2026 9:59am\nL10: \nL11: 【5†Bayer】 【6†Cell & Gene Therapy】 【7†Pompe disease】 【8†rare diseases】\nL12: \nL13: 【9†Image: pipeline, pipes, R&D, research†qtxasset.com】 While Bayer had the option to keep two Pompe disease programs running simultaneously, the company has discontinued ACTUS-101. (blacklight_trace/iStock/Getty Images Plus)\nL14: Bayer has discontinued an early-stage clinical gene therapy for a rare genetic disorder in favor of a similar candidate.\nL15: \nL16: Bayer acquired a pair of adeno-associated virus gene therapies aimed at late-onset Pompe disease as part of its 【10†$2 billion acquisition】 of AskBio in 2020. Pompe disease is a rare, severe autosomal recessive metabolic disease characterized by progressive muscular degeneration.\nL17: One of the gene therapies, dubbed ACTUS-101, was already in the clinic at the time of that deal. But the future of the asset looked in doubt in January when Bayer explained it was halting further enrollment in that phase 1 study. A total of seven patients had been enrolled in the trial, according to the 【11†federal trials database†clinicaltrials.gov】.\nL18: Bayer 【12†provided that update†www.bayer.com】 in the context of ramping up work on its other Pompe disease gene therapy, dubbed AB-1009. The German pharma took the candidate—which has secured FDA fast track and orphan drug designations—into a phase 1/2 trial in the U.S. earlier this year.\nL19: “This investigational gene therapy is being studied for its potential to address the underlying genetic defect and to explore whether it can increase production of the deficient enzyme in patients with Pompe disease,” Tahseen Mozaffar, M.D., director of the UCI Health ALS & Neuromuscular Center and the principal investigator for the AB-1009 program, explained in the January release.\nL20: While Bayer had the option to keep both Pompe disease programs running simultaneously, the company confirmed in its 【13†fourth-quarter earnings documents†www.bayer.com】 (PDF) that ACTUS-101 has now been discontinued.\nL21: ### Related\nL22: \nL23: 【14†FDA slaps hold on Denali's plans for phase 1 rare disease trial】\nL24: \nL25: Other biopharmas have struggled carrying their Pompe candidates through the clinic. Denali Therapeutics' enzyme replacement therapy (ERT) was 【14†placed on hold】 by the FDA in December, with the regulator citing concerns about immune reactions in preclinical mouse studies. Back in 2024, 【15†Roche abandoned】 a Pompe gene therapy after weighing up the competitive landscape.\nL26: At the time, Roche noted that an established standard of care exists for Pompe disease in the 【16†form of various approved ERTs†www.fiercepharma.com】.\nL27: \nL28: “While there are no currently approved gene therapies for Pompe disease, investigational treatments are being evaluated by different organizations,” Roche explained at the time.\nL29: Astellas has its own Pompe gene therapy in the works, although that was also 【17†placed on clinical hold】 for the second half of 2022 while the FDA investigated a report of nerve damage in one of the trial’s participants. The regulatory hold was lifted in early 2023.\nL30: \nL31: 【5†Bayer】 【6†Cell & Gene Therapy】 【7†Pompe disease】 【8†rare diseases】 【18†gene therapy】 【3†Biotech】\nL32: \nL33: ©2026 Questex LLC All rights reserved.\nL34: \nL35: 【19†Terms of use†questex.com】\nL36: \nL37: 【20†Privacy Policy†questex.com】\nL38: \nL39: 【21†Privacy Settings】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRoche Drops Pompe Gene Therapy Program | CGTlive® (https://www.cgtlive.com/view/roche-drops-pompe-gene-therapy-program)\nciteturn686view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.cgtlive.com/view/roche-drops-pompe-gene-therapy-program\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\nL0: [Input: Search...]\nL1: \nL2: 【0†Home】\nL3: \nL4: 【1†News】\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Media】\nL7: \nL8: 【2†All Videos】【3†Clinical Trials in Progress】【4†ImmunoLogic】【5†News Network】\nL9: \nL10: 【6†Partners】\nL11: \nL12: More\nL13: \nL14: [Input: Search...]\nL15: \nL16:   * 【0†Home】\nL17:   * 【1†News】\nL18:   * 【2†Media】\nL19: \nL20:   * 【6†Partners】\nL21:   * 【7†CME/CE】\nL22:   * 【8†Conferences】\nL23:   * 【9†Sponsored】\nL24:   * 【10†Subscribe】\nL25: \nL26:   * 【11†Cardiology】\nL27:   * 【12†Dermatology】\nL28:   * 【13†Endocrinology】\nL29:   * 【14†Hematology】\nL30:   * 【15†Lysosomal Disorders】\nL31:   * 【16†Neurology】\nL32:   * 【17†Oncology】\nL33:   * 【18†Ophthalmology】\nL34: \nL35: Choose Specialty\nL36: News|Articles|July 26, 2024\nL37: # Roche Drops Pompe Gene Therapy Program\nL38: \nL39: Author(s)【19†Victoria Johnson】\nL40: \nL41: The company acquired the program, along with Spark Therapeutics’ pipeline, in December 2019.\nL42: \nL43: Roche has discontinued development of its investigational gene therapy SPK-3006, which was being evaluated in the phase 1/2 RESOLUTE trial (NCT04093349) in participants with 【20†late-onset Pompe disease】.^{1}\nL44: Roche adopted the program into its pipeline along with its acquisition of Spark Therapeutics in December 2019.^{2} The trial dosed its first participant in February 2021 and no data updates have been shared from the trial since.^{3}\nL45: The quiet discontinuation comes a year after the company made a similar move with another of its acquired programs from Spark, SPK-8016 gene therapy, which was also being evaluated in a phase 1/2 trial (NCT03734588) for participants with hemophilia A and Factor VIII inhibitors.^{4} Spark now has 1 gene therapy remaining in its pipeline, SPK-8011 (dirloctocogene samoparvovec), which previously 【21†showed some promise】 in participants with hemophilia A in a phase 1/2 trial (NCT03003533) and which is currently being evaluated in the phase 3 KEYSTONE 1 trial (NCT06297486).^{5} Notably, Spark also collaborated on the development of 【22†fidanacogene elaparvovec】, Pfizer’s gene therapy approved for treating moderate to severe hemophilia B in April 2024.^{6}\nL46: READ MORE: 【23†News and Expert Insights in Pompe Disease Gene Therapy for International Pompe Day】\nL47: “With up to 5 years of follow-up after a single infusion of SPK-8011 we see a result of durable FVIII expression with the majority of participants experiencing levels in the mild hemophilia range.\nL48: Investigations are ongoing to overcome the challenge of managing a presumed capsid immune response to further reduce variability in FVIII expression following AAV-mediated gene transfer for hemophilia A,” Stacy Croteau, MD, MMS, Medical Director, Boston Hemophilia Center, and Assistant Professor of Pediatrics, Harvard Medical School, said while presenting the phase 1/2 trial data on SPK-8011.^{5}\nL49: Roche’s withdrawal from the Pompe space leaves behind active players such as Astellas Pharma and Actus Bio, which are developing the gene therapies 【24†AT845】 and 【20†ACTUS-101】, respectively.\nL50: \nL51: AT845 is currently being evaluated in the phase 1/2 FORTIS clinical trial (NCT04174105), in which 4 participants with LOPD have demonstrated stable forced vital capacity over time following treatment with AT845, including after enzyme replacement therapy (ERT) withdrawal in 3 participants as of February 2023.^{7}\nL52: ACTUS-101 is being evaluated for the same indication in another phase 1/2 clinical trial (NCT03533673), the most recent data from which were presented in May 2022. All participants met criteria for ERT withdrawal at week 24 and continued to meet criteria to remain off ERT at weeks 52 and 104, although 1 participant chose to resume ERT at week 97.^{8}\nL53: ###### REFERENCES\nL54: ###### 1. HY 2024 results. Roche. Presentation. July 25, 2024.\nL55: 2. Keown A. The Wait Is Over: After Multiple Delays, Roche Completes Acquisition of Spark Therapeutics. December 17, 2019. Article. https://www.biospace.com/after-multiple-delays-roche-completes-acquisition-of-spark-therapeutics\nL56: 3. Spark Therapeutics Announces First Participant Dosed in Phase 1/2 Study of Investigational Gene Therapy for Late-Onset Pompe Disease. News release. Spark Therapeutics. February 1, 2021. https://sparktx.com/press_releases/first-patient-dosed-pompe/\nL57: 4. HY 2023 results. Roche. Presentation. July 27, 2023.\nL58: 5. Long-term durable FVIII expression with improvements in bleeding rates following AAV-mediated FVIII gene transfer for hemophilia A: Multiyear follow-up on the phase I/II trial of SPK-8011. Presented at: 64th ASH Annual Meeting, December 10-13, 2022. Abstract #783\nL59: 6. U.S. FDA Approves Pfizer’s BEQVEZ™ (fidanacogene elaparvovec-dzkt), a One-Time Gene Therapy for Adults with Hemophilia B. News release. Pfizer. April 26, 2024. Accessed April 26, 2024. https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20240425269649/en/U.S.-FDA-Approves-Pfizer%E2%80%99s-BEQVEZ%E2%84%A2-fidanacogene-elaparvovec-dzkt-a-One-Time-Gene-Therapy-for-Adults-with-Hemophilia-B\nL60: 7. Diaz-Manera J, Mozaffar T, Longo N, et al. AT845 gene replacement therapy for late onset Pompe disease: An update on safety and preliminary efficacy data from FORTIS, a phase I/II open-label clinical study. Presented at WORLDSymposium 2023, held February 22-26, in Orlando, Florida. Abstract #95\nL61: 8. Smith EC, Hopkins S, Case LE, et al. Phase 1 Study of Gene Therapy in Late-onset Pompe Disease: Initial 104-Week Experience. Presented at: ASGCT 25th Annual Meeting; May 16-19, 2022; Washington DC. Abstract #1211\nL62: ### Newsletter\nL63: \nL64: Stay at the forefront of cutting-edge science with CGT—your direct line to expert insights, breakthrough data, and real-time coverage of the latest advancements in cell and gene therapy.\nL65: \nL66: 【10†Subscribe】\nL67: \nL68: 【15†Lysosomal Disorders】\nL69: \nL70: * * *\nL71: ## Related to this article\nL72: \nL73: 【1†View more】\nL74: \nL75: September 2nd, 2026\nL76: \nL77: 【25†Novartis, BMS Pause Autoimmune CAR-T Trials After Safety Events】\nL78: \nL79: Novartis suspended several autoimmune and neurology trials of rap-cel after 3 patient deaths linked to immune effector cell-associated hemophagocytic syndrome, and BMS separately paused zola-cel studies after observing inflammatory safety events.\nL80: \nL81: 【25†Read more】\nL82: \nL83: August 24th, 2026\nL84: \nL85: 【26†PTC Wins Sangamo's Fabry Gene Therapy in Bankruptcy Auction】\nL86: PTC Therapeutics wins Sangamo's BLA-stage Fabry disease gene therapy ST-920 for $111 million in a bankruptcy auction, with STAAR data supporting accelerated approval.\nL87: \nL88: 【26†Read more】\nL89: \nL90: August 20th, 2026\nL91: \nL92: 【27†Pariglasgene Brecaparvovec-opnr Approved for Glycogen Storage Disease 1a】\nL93: \nL94: FDA granted accelerated approval to pariglasgene brecaparvovec-opnr, a one-time liver-directed AAV8 gene therapy, based on a 31% reduction in cornstarch intake versus placebo.\nL95: \nL96: 【27†Read more】\nL97: \nL98: May 27th, 2026\nL99: 【28†Around the Helix: Cell and Gene Therapy Company Updates – May 27, 2026】\nL100: \nL101: Catch up on the latest news, breakthroughs, and announcements from biotechnology companies making advancements in cell and gene therapies.\nL102: \nL103: 【28†Read more】\nL104: \nL105: May 20th, 2026\nL106: \nL107: 【29†Around the Helix: Cell and Gene Therapy Company Updates – May 20, 2026】\nL108: \nL109: Catch up on the latest news, breakthroughs, a